00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:01.730
Good day, everyone.

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:05.620
This is Jack Van Horn from the University
of Southern California, and I'd like to welcome

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:09.070
you
to the Big Data to Knowledge Guide to the

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:10.160
Fundamentals of Data Science.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:17.449
We're very pleased to have Ravi Madduri speaking
with us today on the subject of Big Data Technologies

00:00:17.449 --> 00:00:20.179
For
Biomedical Knowledge Discovery.

00:00:20.179 --> 00:00:24.479
Ravi Madduri is a senior computational scientist
based at the University of

00:00:24.479 --> 00:00:27.419
Chicago as well as the Argonne National Laboratory.

00:00:27.419 --> 00:00:30.759
His research interests are in large scale
computation and

00:00:30.759 --> 00:00:35.920
data management, and he leads the Globist
Genomics Project, which uses genomics, proteomics,

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.989
and other
biomedical computations on cloud-based and

00:00:38.989 --> 00:00:40.850
other HCP platforms.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:45.980
He received an Outstanding Achievement
Award by the NIH in 2007 for the creation

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:49.540
of tools for data analysis in cancer biology.

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:54.590
And recently he participated
in the Vice President's Cancer Moonshot Project,

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:57.570
receiving an award from the Secretary of the
US Department of

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:58.570
Energy.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:02.659
We're very pleased to have Ravi with us this
morning talking on Big Data Technologies For

00:01:02.659 --> 00:01:07.860
Biomedical Knowledge
Discovery, and I encourage you in any questions

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:11.250
you have, either go to webinar questions submission
system,

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:17.850
they are located in your go-to webinar panel
off to the right on your screen.

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.659
And we'll get to some of those at the
end.

00:01:20.659 --> 00:01:23.270
Without further ado, allow me to introduce
Ravi.

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:25.170
Ravi, the stage is yours.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.180
Thank you, Jack.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:28.659
Thank you for that kind introduction.

00:01:28.659 --> 00:01:33.810
And thank you and [? Krystal ?] for having
me here.

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:41.440
One of the-- so let me get started.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:50.149
So, I kind of wanted to take some time to
go over why we talk about big data in life

00:01:50.149 --> 00:01:56.090
sciences and why it is
important, especially because a lot of times

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:02.039
researchers that work in this domain present
their results in a manner

00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:12.970
that folks sometimes don't understand, why
investigation and investment into this kind

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:18.070
of infrastructure, this kind of
analysis, is important.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:23.850
I'll talk a little bit about what are some
of the tools that we've dealt with as part

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:30.110
of the NIH Big Data to Knowledge
Center, called BDDS, and how we are developing

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:39.390
these tools 
to create an impact and to accelerate that

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:45.010
discovery
process and increase our understanding on

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:51.250
a genetic basis for various neurogenic degenerative
disorders.

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:57.970
I'll put
a little bit more emphasis on the data management

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:02.420
and data analysis that these tools have enabled
in the context

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:08.880
of big data, and kind of end with some of
the results that we have generated using these

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:09.880
data sets.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.970
And in the
end, I think Jack and [? Krystal ?] will come

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:17.030
back online to open up the floor for questions,
and I'll be happy to

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:19.590
answer those questions.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:29.340
So, let me take a few minutes and kind of
go over why I personally think this is important.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:35.780
As Jack mentioned in his
introduction, I'm a computational scientist.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:41.440
I'm a senior scientist at the University of
Chicago and Argonne National

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:42.700
Laboratory.

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:44.550
We have supercomputers.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:49.910
We have large-scale, high-performance computing
resources here.

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:55.760
And
why do I, as a computer scientist, find this

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.540
kind of Big Data Analysis in [INAUDIBLE] important?

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:04.890
And I'll have a few
slides where I'll present, at a high level,

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:08.820
why what we do is important.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:19.289
So I took a little chart from the CDC, which
every year they present some of the leading

00:04:19.289 --> 00:04:24.539
causes of death in the US,
and if you look at the chart on the left,

00:04:24.539 --> 00:04:32.509
you see that the top two leading causes of
death are heart disease and

00:04:32.509 --> 00:04:36.580
cancer.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:43.449
The provocative statement that I want to make
here is that a good portion of these deaths

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:49.379
could be
preventable if they were detected early on,

00:04:49.379 --> 00:04:54.510
or if we understood them better and were able
to see if there are any

00:04:54.510 --> 00:05:00.430
therapeutics that that could be developed
or that are already existing that could be

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:06.069
re-used to lower the suffering
or to lower the deaths that are caused by

00:05:06.069 --> 00:05:07.990
these diseases.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:16.680
And one caveat that I want to say is that,
with the increase in the understanding of

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:21.750
how these diseases propagate
in the human body, there is a strong evidence

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:27.960
that shows that there is a pretty good genetic
basis for these

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:28.960
diseases.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:41.469
So there is something in a human gene that
either causes these diseases or has a key

00:05:41.469 --> 00:05:46.990
to reduce the
suffering that folks have to endure when they

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:47.990
get these diseases.

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:57.080
So, I do understand that I'm making a sort
of a blanket argument, because each of these,

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:01.860
heart disease and
cancer, research tells us they are not one

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:07.460
disease, there are actually multiple variations
of this disease.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:10.569
Just within
heart disease, there are three types.

00:06:10.569 --> 00:06:17.090
At least on a broader level, there are multiple
subtypes of these types.

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:21.090
And
and maybe folks know a bit more about cancer.

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:25.699
Cancer is in fact a collection of 100 or so
different types of

00:06:25.699 --> 00:06:30.069
diseases that are grouped under one umbrella.

00:06:30.069 --> 00:06:39.060
So, as you can see there's a big motivation
for why we have to work in this area.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:47.439
And I'll talk a little bit about how
this leads into Big Data, and how Big Data

00:06:47.439 --> 00:06:51.620
analysis and Big Data management will help
us get more

00:06:51.620 --> 00:07:01.180
understanding about how we can make a dent
this in these disease areas.

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:09.659
So, this is a paper that was published in
Nature, and the authors make a statement,

00:07:09.659 --> 00:07:14.110
and Mark, Dr. Rubin, is at
Cardinal Medical College in New York, and

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:21.150
they say that they found out after a lot of
data analysis that 90% of

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:31.309
people who are diagnosed with a type of cancer
do carry a genetic variation of mutation that

00:07:31.309 --> 00:07:35.550
makes these patients
responsive to a known drug.

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:38.500
So let me kind of take a minute to let this
sink in.

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:43.800
So 90% of people who are suffering from cancer
that are out

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:53.240
there carry a genetic mutation that makes
these people respond to a known drug.

00:07:53.240 --> 00:08:00.150
So we could be helping them if
we know what mutation this is that people

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:02.110
carry.

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:07.669
Finding out what mutation that people who
are diagnosed with

00:08:07.669 --> 00:08:14.999
cancer carry, I argue, is a Big Data analysis
problem, and these are some of the tools that

00:08:14.999 --> 00:08:21.339
we developed to make
it easy for us to discover the mutations that

00:08:21.339 --> 00:08:28.990
these patients carry that may lead to prescribing
a drug that would

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:31.430
help them a little bit.

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:38.240
So that's in cancer, and if you move to the
cardiovascular diseases and a study that was

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:42.020
published in the New
England Journal of Medicine where they they've

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:46.040
done a pretty comprehensive study up of over
55,000

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:58.530
participants, in which they looked into the
various factors that make people susceptible

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:05.680
to different heart diseases.

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:14.630
If you look at this paper, it's pretty clear
that lifestyle choices do definitely have

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:21.770
the power to reduce your genetic
risk to these diseases.

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:27.810
But in the context of this talk the point
I'm trying to make here is that in the cardiovascular

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:36.320
disease area there's
been a lot of signs that help discover the

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:42.220
genetic risk factors that may make a person
more susceptible to the

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:44.740
coronary heart diseases.

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:56.950
So, as you can see in the top two killers
in the US, cancer and heart disease, we see

00:09:56.950 --> 00:10:02.650
an
increase in our understanding of how various

00:10:02.650 --> 00:10:08.760
factors, genetic factors and lifestyle factors
that affect these

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:17.680
diseases, would help us create therapeutics
or take existing drugs and, by being able

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:22.850
to discover what would be
effective for a particular subject, we can

00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:30.720
actually do a great deal of good to folks
who are suffering or who might

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:34.020
be suffering from this disease.

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:42.470
This particular thinking is often termed as
precision medicine, where you understand the

00:10:42.470 --> 00:10:51.650
individual risk profile and
you tailor their treatment to match their

00:10:51.650 --> 00:11:05.920
genetic and other lifestyle factors that fit
that profile.

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:10.740
So Nancy Cox, the president of the American
Society of Human Genetics and longtime collaborator

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:19.040
for me, says
that in order to find a single mutation or

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:24.260
a bunch of mutations or genetic variants that
may make a person more

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:31.940
susceptible to cancer or coronary heart disease,
finding those mutations or finding those genetic

00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:39.920
variants is akin to
finding a needle in a haystack, meaning that

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:45.490
you're essentially sifting through a lot of
data to find this one

00:11:45.490 --> 00:12:01.280
particular data or evidence that may throw
more light into understanding a person's susceptibility

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:07.900
or factors that
may have affected this particular person's

00:12:07.900 --> 00:12:11.390
disease onset.

00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:20.340
So one of the things that folks in this area
of science do is, in order to find this needle

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:24.870
in a haystack, people
typically build their own infrastructure.

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:34.740
They build huge computing farms called private
clusters that take a lot of

00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:37.430
time and money to build.

00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:48.230
So in some sense, folks are building their
own haystacks, or sorting machines.

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:57.630
So one of the things that we argue in this
presentation is that by building your own

00:12:57.630 --> 00:13:02.651
infrastructure, by building your
own needle haystack-sorting machine, you're

00:13:02.651 --> 00:13:09.060
essentially spending valuable time and money
in creating

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:17.920
infrastructure, whereas our goal in the BDDS
project is to build services and tools that

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:23.240
will hopefully prevent you or
a researcher from having to build their own

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:28.610
infrastructure and their own tools to find
these needles in a haystack.

00:13:28.610 --> 00:13:37.040
So these needles could be a protein that you
would be able to find using large scale proteomics

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:41.840
analysis, or these
needles could be a genetic marker that you

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:46.830
could find using large-scale genomic analysis.

00:13:46.830 --> 00:13:52.760
And in this particular
talk, I'll take an example of a hypothesis

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:57.800
that our colleagues in this project from the
Institute for Systems Biology in

00:13:57.800 --> 00:14:08.440
Seattle have made, which is to get a better
understanding of transcription regulative

00:14:08.440 --> 00:14:14.620
factors that affect a gene to
express itself more.

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:19.390
Once we understand these transcription factors
better, we'll be able to build a network of

00:14:19.390 --> 00:14:25.460
them that will help shed more light on diseases
like Alzheimer's and others.

00:14:25.460 --> 00:14:38.690
So one of the things that I also want to emphasize
is that the needle in a haystack analogy is

00:14:38.690 --> 00:14:43.100
getting more and
more appropriate if you look at the growth

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:52.590
in how much human genetic data we're collecting.

00:14:52.590 --> 00:15:00.410
So this little graph
kind of has become a must-show in all of the

00:15:00.410 --> 00:15:07.940
Big Data talks because it shows how the genetic
data is growing.

00:15:07.940 --> 00:15:15.500
I keep thinking about a day when I can present
a talk without having to show this slide,

00:15:15.500 --> 00:15:20.750
because it's kind of a little
scary and depressing because we are in no

00:15:20.750 --> 00:15:29.380
way equipped to handle the data that we are
expected to generate in

00:15:29.380 --> 00:15:31.590
sequencing DNA.

00:15:31.590 --> 00:15:39.310
As you can see, one of the things that people
say is that we're collecting more data than

00:15:39.310 --> 00:15:43.250
we
have available storage in the world.

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:50.750
But anyway, so this is a chart that shows
how much genetic data is growing.

00:15:50.750 --> 00:16:01.850
And this is a little map that I put together
to show locations in the world where genetic

00:16:01.850 --> 00:16:07.360
sequencers are operational
and are generating data.

00:16:07.360 --> 00:16:16.590
So you might think that all of the data that
is being generated in genetics is US-centric

00:16:16.590 --> 00:16:21.740
or
maybe generated at one place, but in fact

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:23.090
it's not.

00:16:23.090 --> 00:16:26.370
All of these machines, the sequencing machines,
are distributed

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:31.670
geographically across the world, and all of
them are generating data.

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:37.030
So one of the new sequencing machines that
Illumina has released has a potential to generate

00:16:37.030 --> 00:16:40.340
a terabyte of data
every day.

00:16:40.340 --> 00:16:46.720
So if you can multiply that with the numbers
here, you can see how we are at an increased

00:16:46.720 --> 00:16:49.790
rate at
which we can generate the data.

00:16:49.790 --> 00:16:58.400
So the graph that I showed in the earlier
slide in fact could have a bigger slope

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:06.260
or we could be trending there much faster.

00:17:06.260 --> 00:17:15.429
So if you look at why finding the needles
in a haystack or why finding insights In Big

00:17:15.429 --> 00:17:25.850
Data is a challenge, you can
see-- we looked at this, we analyzed these

00:17:25.850 --> 00:17:28.429
challenges for a long time.

00:17:28.429 --> 00:17:33.549
This has been our area of research for 20
years now.

00:17:33.549 --> 00:17:42.110
When we looked at some of the fundamental
challenges that researchers faced in doing

00:17:42.110 --> 00:17:45.990
this kind of
research, there are two sets of challenges.

00:17:45.990 --> 00:17:51.220
One is in the area of data management and
access challenges.

00:17:51.220 --> 00:17:59.510
In genomics and proteomics, in a lot of our
research where people are trying to find these

00:17:59.510 --> 00:18:04.370
proverbial needles, data that they need or
that they are generating

00:18:04.370 --> 00:18:07.539
is typically distributed across the world.

00:18:07.539 --> 00:18:13.470
So if you take the example of genetics data,
there are reference data sets

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:27.009
that are being used that are located in different
places and sequencing centers that are generating

00:18:27.009 --> 00:18:29.899
this data for
researchers that I showed in the previous

00:18:29.899 --> 00:18:35.809
slide, they are shipping hard drives full
of genetic data to researchers.

00:18:35.809 --> 00:18:41.970
And it's a very inefficient way of moving
data.

00:18:41.970 --> 00:18:48.350
Because if you try to move a large data set
using FedEx or one of

00:18:48.350 --> 00:18:55.899
the older internet protocols call FTP or HTP,
you'd realize that this process takes multiple

00:18:55.899 --> 00:19:00.919
days to move the data.

00:19:00.919 --> 00:19:07.960
Researchers typically rely on sharing data
with each other when they collaborate and

00:19:07.960 --> 00:19:13.110
there's this collaboration,
since sharing typically happens by putting

00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:15.690
a web server and putting the data there.

00:19:15.690 --> 00:19:22.950
Again, these are very
inefficient ways of sharing data with your

00:19:22.950 --> 00:19:25.539
collaborators.

00:19:25.539 --> 00:19:33.090
The reason why people generate data, the primary
reason, is they want to analyze this data.

00:19:33.090 --> 00:19:37.100
And when they have
to analyze the data it could be using a cloud

00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:41.840
computing resource or it could be using a
supercomputer.

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:44.419
Typically
what happens is researchers have to move the

00:19:44.419 --> 00:19:49.090
data again into a local compute capability.

00:19:49.090 --> 00:19:53.539
So as you can see there's a lot of movement
of data.

00:19:53.539 --> 00:19:58.519
And you could say that oh, FedEx is very good
at moving

00:19:58.519 --> 00:20:00.090
data.

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:09.220
I worked with a researcher who got their sequencing
done in China and who FedEx'ed their drive

00:20:09.220 --> 00:20:11.030
full of
genomics data.

00:20:11.030 --> 00:20:17.119
It got stuck in customs because they wanted
to test it to make sure it passed through.

00:20:17.119 --> 00:20:19.999
So it takes a long time for people to get
data.

00:20:19.999 --> 00:20:24.440
So once you have the data, you can see the
next set of challenges

00:20:24.440 --> 00:20:29.619
in the analysis of this data, because that's
where you get the insights that you're looking

00:20:29.619 --> 00:20:30.619
for.

00:20:30.619 --> 00:20:33.850
And people typically
resort to writing shell scripts.

00:20:33.850 --> 00:20:38.730
Arguably, these are people who are computationally
sophisticated, and they are

00:20:38.730 --> 00:20:42.840
writing good shell scripts to do their analysis.

00:20:42.840 --> 00:20:49.070
But if you look at the shell scripts there's
a lot of manual processes in

00:20:49.070 --> 00:20:50.070
this analysis.

00:20:50.070 --> 00:20:55.950
And whenever you have manual processes, you
know that it could be made more efficient.

00:20:55.950 --> 00:21:01.950
So if you look at the analysis side, what
people do is typically manually move all the

00:21:01.950 --> 00:21:06.519
data to where they could do
the analysis, they install all the tools,

00:21:06.519 --> 00:21:12.580
and they sequentially run this analysis on
their compute clusters.

00:21:12.580 --> 00:21:20.950
And one of
the main disadvantages for this is that, because

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:25.139
they are done by an individual or a team,
they're hard to

00:21:25.139 --> 00:21:26.159
reproduce.

00:21:26.159 --> 00:21:34.470
The knowledge that is gained is kind of lost,
or is just only available in that group.

00:21:34.470 --> 00:21:39.440
So oftentimes the shell scripts are like toothbrushes.

00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:42.330
You can't share toothbrushes and you can share
shell

00:21:42.330 --> 00:21:43.570
scripts.

00:21:43.570 --> 00:21:49.249
So it's a pretty simple analogy.

00:21:49.249 --> 00:21:55.639
So the shell scripts and the data management,
manually moving data and

00:21:55.639 --> 00:22:01.691
manually making the data available to your
collaborators and doing the analysis, it's

00:22:01.691 --> 00:22:08.149
a cumbersome process that
often leads to science that is not reproducible.

00:22:08.149 --> 00:22:12.289
When science is not reproducible, it makes
it harder for somebody

00:22:12.289 --> 00:22:17.179
to validate your findings and build upon those
findings.

00:22:17.179 --> 00:22:23.830
So what that means is that if I am a researcher
and I want to leverage somebody else's work,

00:22:23.830 --> 00:22:29.159
and I want to build
on top of it, I can't do that because the

00:22:29.159 --> 00:22:37.279
tools that you've used are making this into
a process that is not reliable to

00:22:37.279 --> 00:22:38.410
be purchasable.

00:22:38.410 --> 00:22:49.370
So within the BDDS, this is a little graphic
that kind of shows why having Big Data or

00:22:49.370 --> 00:22:55.840
generating
Big Data may be very overwhelming for a researcher

00:22:55.840 --> 00:22:57.019
to do the analysis.

00:22:57.019 --> 00:23:03.720
What we want to do is we want to
harness the power of Big Data, and to lead

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:09.529
that power into ideas and to validation into
new findings.

00:23:09.529 --> 00:23:19.700
So my goal here today is to show you how,
in the BDDS center, we are generating these

00:23:19.700 --> 00:23:25.769
tools that allow you to
harness the power of Big Data in a way that

00:23:25.769 --> 00:23:30.690
doesn't overwhelm you, and in a way that this
power could lead to

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:34.990
ideas and discoveries.

00:23:34.990 --> 00:23:43.789
So in our center, our guiding principle is
to let the data lead you to the discoveries.

00:23:43.789 --> 00:23:49.809
So we know that there's been a
lot of effort and time and resources spent

00:23:49.809 --> 00:23:52.870
in generating a lot of data resources.

00:23:52.870 --> 00:23:59.639
I'll talk a little bit about some of
these data resources and how big they are.

00:23:59.639 --> 00:24:03.429
But it's not enough to just generate the data.

00:24:03.429 --> 00:24:11.119
It's also important to
harness this data in a way that is reproducible

00:24:11.119 --> 00:24:14.860
and to deliver and generate results.

00:24:14.860 --> 00:24:25.649
So in BDDS we created a platform that allows
researchers to manage the data more efficiently.

00:24:25.649 --> 00:24:32.119
It allows them to
create analysis pipelines and publish them

00:24:32.119 --> 00:24:36.909
in a way such that the whole process is reproducible.

00:24:36.909 --> 00:24:42.840
We created
ways, we created tools, that enable this discovery

00:24:42.840 --> 00:24:45.320
process in a reproducible manner.

00:24:45.320 --> 00:24:49.249
So these are some of the
tools that we developed.

00:24:49.249 --> 00:24:57.899
I'll talk a little bit about each one of them
with an emphasis on one particular scientific

00:24:57.899 --> 00:25:03.700
hypothesis that will be validated.

00:25:03.700 --> 00:25:10.540
So the BDDS platform, as I mentioned before,
has tools in two different categories.

00:25:10.540 --> 00:25:16.509
One is the data management
category that makes it easy for researchers

00:25:16.509 --> 00:25:21.629
to integrate data sets from genomics, proteomics,
and image

00:25:21.629 --> 00:25:26.899
datasets, and then end-to-end a standard format
so that you can exchange these datasets with

00:25:26.899 --> 00:25:30.940
your collaborators
with enough metadata so they can know exactly

00:25:30.940 --> 00:25:36.080
where this data is coming from and what this
data contains in it.

00:25:36.080 --> 00:25:42.980
We created tools and services that allow researchers
to create reusable processes.

00:25:42.980 --> 00:25:50.840
We created catalogs to
resource, catalogs of data for researchers

00:25:50.840 --> 00:25:53.879
to discover what results have been created.

00:25:53.879 --> 00:26:00.340
We created interfaces for
researchers to easily create cohorts, so for

00:26:00.340 --> 00:26:06.299
example you want to say I want to do this
analysis on all the patients

00:26:06.299 --> 00:26:11.350
that have this particular genetic marker,
or all the patients that are diagnosed with

00:26:11.350 --> 00:26:17.509
lung cancer that haven't
smoked in their lives, so we created tools

00:26:17.509 --> 00:26:22.309
that allow you to create these cohorts.

00:26:22.309 --> 00:26:28.200
Finally we, created and operate
these services that make it easy for you to

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:34.519
rapidly analyze these data sets on public
cloud computing resources,

00:26:34.519 --> 00:26:37.350
high-performance computing resources.

00:26:37.350 --> 00:26:45.190
The key is to make these analysis pipelines
be able to be reproducible on demand so others

00:26:45.190 --> 00:26:49.790
can build on top of
this these pipelines without having to recreate

00:26:49.790 --> 00:26:51.190
them.

00:26:51.190 --> 00:27:01.419
So this is the hypotheses that we worked on,
which is to get better understanding of what

00:27:01.419 --> 00:27:08.629
are the genetic
susceptibility loci for Alzheimer's disease?

00:27:08.629 --> 00:27:15.009
There's some meta-analysis that's been done
or for 74,000 individuals.

00:27:15.009 --> 00:27:22.129
And we really, once we started looking at
the data, realized how little we really know.

00:27:22.129 --> 00:27:26.390
There's been a lot of
literature on this transcriptomic regulatory

00:27:26.390 --> 00:27:30.980
factors that do affect the genetic expression.

00:27:30.980 --> 00:27:39.960
So we wanted to
understand how much these transcriptomic factors,

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:48.609
how much more insight these factors can provide
us in the

00:27:48.609 --> 00:27:50.730
onset of Alzheimer's.

00:27:50.730 --> 00:27:59.100
So our colleague said, yes, we have created
a method called TReNA which tries to create

00:27:59.100 --> 00:28:02.929
a network out of this
transcriptomic regulatory factors that helps

00:28:02.929 --> 00:28:10.539
you better reason why some of these factors
do have an impact on

00:28:10.539 --> 00:28:13.450
Alzheimer's disease.

00:28:13.450 --> 00:28:21.379
One of the ideas is to create a model for
every gene that you identify in this analysis,

00:28:21.379 --> 00:28:24.820
and
use the transcript from related factors to

00:28:24.820 --> 00:28:33.299
better explain why you see the onset of these
diseases in these subjects.

00:28:33.299 --> 00:28:44.940
So this hypothesis has resulted in a very
comprehensive flow chart that describes radius

00:28:44.940 --> 00:28:48.730
datasets and radius
analysis that we need to perform in order

00:28:48.730 --> 00:28:51.779
to get the insight that we are talking about.

00:28:51.779 --> 00:28:57.299
This pipeline was so
complicated that it didn't fit into a single

00:28:57.299 --> 00:29:01.669
slide, even though I spent a considerable
amount of time, as you can see.

00:29:01.669 --> 00:29:07.070
You saw this important factor box is lost.

00:29:07.070 --> 00:29:14.649
But the idea here is that we have a series
of analysis pipelines that we need to develop.

00:29:14.649 --> 00:29:19.149
And we have a few
datasets that are publicly available that

00:29:19.149 --> 00:29:23.869
are generated by the NIH, including the ENCODE
dataset and the

00:29:23.869 --> 00:29:31.749
Roadmap dataset, that have all of the raw
data that we need to analyze in order to generate

00:29:31.749 --> 00:29:36.369
this regulatory
networks that better explain the onset of

00:29:36.369 --> 00:29:41.429
Alzheimer's in the subjects that I mentioned
before.

00:29:41.429 --> 00:29:50.759
So this is a pipeline, a set of analyses that
we developed, and we created a new tool called

00:29:50.759 --> 00:29:56.210
TReNA which takes
results from all of this analysis and gives

00:29:56.210 --> 00:30:04.489
you more evidence on, or better explanation
for, various genetic factors,

00:30:04.489 --> 00:30:15.159
or of the proverbial needles in a haystack
we try to find using these techniques.

00:30:15.159 --> 00:30:25.590
So I divided this into a few categories, and
data discovery is-- so as I described in one

00:30:25.590 --> 00:30:33.559
of the slides before where
we divided this problem into a set of categories

00:30:33.559 --> 00:30:36.419
--one category is discovery, right?

00:30:36.419 --> 00:30:42.489
So as I mentioned, there are a
lot of existing NIH-funded databases that

00:30:42.489 --> 00:30:47.119
have the data that we need to do this analysis.

00:30:47.119 --> 00:30:51.090
One of those datasets is
called ENCODE.

00:30:51.090 --> 00:30:58.929
ENCODE is a publicly available dataset which
is an encyclopedia of DNA elements.

00:30:58.929 --> 00:31:03.480
It has a
publicly available interface, a web portal

00:31:03.480 --> 00:31:06.299
that you can go and query for the data.

00:31:06.299 --> 00:31:14.519
And, as you can see, it has a lot
of tissue samples that we need to do this

00:31:14.519 --> 00:31:17.259
analysis.

00:31:17.259 --> 00:31:26.399
One of the first things we did in order to
make it easy for us was to create these portable

00:31:26.399 --> 00:31:30.259
data bags.

00:31:30.259 --> 00:31:36.429
We call this
tool a BDBag, which is essentially-- as I

00:31:36.429 --> 00:31:42.159
mentioned before, there are multiple pipelines
in this discovery process,

00:31:42.159 --> 00:31:49.950
and we want to make it easy for us to move
that data from one analysis too, one platform,

00:31:49.950 --> 00:31:51.820
to the next platform.

00:31:51.820 --> 00:31:54.929
In
order for us to do that, we had to create

00:31:54.929 --> 00:32:02.029
a mechanism to make it easy for us to portably
move datasets, metadata,

00:32:02.029 --> 00:32:07.009
so that for every pipeline we don't have to
recreate the datasets and regenerate them

00:32:07.009 --> 00:32:09.279
in order for us to do the
analysis.

00:32:09.279 --> 00:32:18.100
So one of the things that we did was we looked
at how the Library of Congress handles data

00:32:18.100 --> 00:32:20.669
archiving and data
sharing.

00:32:20.669 --> 00:32:27.749
We looked at a product specification called
BagIt, and we extended that specification

00:32:27.749 --> 00:32:32.659
to Big Data and we
created a BDBag specification that allows

00:32:32.659 --> 00:32:40.230
you to create a portable data format, which
is also an acronym for .pdf,

00:32:40.230 --> 00:32:49.721
for Big Data so that when we query ENCODE
and we generate a huge dataset that we want

00:32:49.721 --> 00:32:54.749
to do the analysis on,
we do so as a BDBag.

00:32:54.749 --> 00:33:02.100
We send this data back into multiple analysis
routines so we have enough metadata that

00:33:02.100 --> 00:33:09.299
will lead to the reproducibility and reuseability
of these assets.

00:33:09.299 --> 00:33:12.159
So the BDBag is a pretty simple concept.

00:33:12.159 --> 00:33:17.130
It has a set of raw datasets.

00:33:17.130 --> 00:33:23.789
It drives what data this bag is carrying.

00:33:23.789 --> 00:33:26.880
And
the most important part of this is that it

00:33:26.880 --> 00:33:32.859
has a checksum, which is computer science-speak
for a fingerprint, right?

00:33:32.859 --> 00:33:40.960
So a checksum is a very unique identifier
that allows a receiver to validate that they

00:33:40.960 --> 00:33:45.200
have received the data
without any corruption.

00:33:45.200 --> 00:33:56.049
So the BDBag contains this portable format
in which you can take data from one platform

00:33:56.049 --> 00:34:03.649
to another and hope that the other platform
can understand and process that data.

00:34:03.649 --> 00:34:10.829
So we created a service on top of ENCODE so
that we can create these BDBags.

00:34:10.829 --> 00:34:14.770
ENCODE has a few tissue
samples.

00:34:14.770 --> 00:34:20.819
So for example, if we want to analyze lung
tissue, we can write a query to ENCODE and

00:34:20.819 --> 00:34:27.669
it will generate a
BDBag full of data from lung tissue that we

00:34:27.669 --> 00:34:29.619
can do the analysis on.

00:34:29.619 --> 00:34:31.640
So now we have two things.

00:34:31.640 --> 00:34:38.720
We have a bag full of data that has enough
metadata associated with it.

00:34:38.720 --> 00:34:42.790
We have a
way to query and generate this bag.

00:34:42.790 --> 00:34:47.470
Now, we move to generating an identifier for
this bag.

00:34:47.470 --> 00:34:54.109
The reason why these
identifiers are important is that these are

00:34:54.109 --> 00:34:59.330
the things that you're recording so that when
you publish your results or

00:34:59.330 --> 00:35:05.109
your pipeline there are identifiers that allows
somebody to validate that yes, this exists

00:35:05.109 --> 00:35:06.490
and this is expected.

00:35:06.490 --> 00:35:10.820
This is
as this research team is claiming it to be.

00:35:10.820 --> 00:35:16.050
So we created a service that allows you to
create these identifiers.

00:35:16.050 --> 00:35:21.990
Every data item that you have seen in this
huge pipeline that I showed before has an

00:35:21.990 --> 00:35:24.320
identifier assigned to it.

00:35:24.320 --> 00:35:26.950
This is a digital identifier.

00:35:26.950 --> 00:35:30.200
You can reference it,
you can share it with your collaborators,

00:35:30.200 --> 00:35:31.750
and you can resolve it.

00:35:31.750 --> 00:35:34.839
This makes very this makes it very easy for
you to

00:35:34.839 --> 00:35:36.650
share Big Data sets.

00:35:36.650 --> 00:35:42.480
When you have a minimal identifier, you just
send that minid to your collaborator and they

00:35:42.480 --> 00:35:43.480
can resolve it.

00:35:43.480 --> 00:35:49.069
This solves the problem that I mentioned before
where you're setting up an FTP sever or some

00:35:49.069 --> 00:35:54.500
other format to make it easy for you to share
data.

00:35:54.500 --> 00:35:59.180
So this shows how you can resolve a minimal
identifier, and more importantly choose the

00:35:59.180 --> 00:36:04.970
metadata that is
available with this identifier.

00:36:04.970 --> 00:36:07.569
So it has a unique identifier.

00:36:07.569 --> 00:36:10.880
We use something called ARK to generate this.

00:36:10.880 --> 00:36:14.980
It shows
when this bag is created, it shows who created

00:36:14.980 --> 00:36:20.910
it, and this identifier next to Kyle has what
is called an Arcade,

00:36:20.910 --> 00:36:26.050
which is a research identifier that uniquely
identifies Kyle as a researcher at the University

00:36:26.050 --> 00:36:27.050
of Chicago.

00:36:27.050 --> 00:36:30.049
It provides
a checksum, and it provides a location in

00:36:30.049 --> 00:36:31.720
which you can download this bag.

00:36:31.720 --> 00:36:35.049
Right now this bag is located on
Amazon.

00:36:35.049 --> 00:36:41.360
You could use Amazon data access mechanisms
to retrieve this bag.

00:36:41.360 --> 00:36:49.119
So in order to make it easy for researchers,
we generated a lot of tools to generate these

00:36:49.119 --> 00:36:50.119
identifiers.

00:36:50.119 --> 00:36:53.039
So now that
we have the bag and we have the identifiers

00:36:53.039 --> 00:36:59.170
that uniquely identifies a bag, now we move
to the next level of how

00:36:59.170 --> 00:37:05.329
do we move this data, and how do we reliably
share these bags with researchers?

00:37:05.329 --> 00:37:11.520
One of the challenges that we have in Big
Data is the bag that we generated was around

00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:13.339
23 terabytes of data,
right?

00:37:13.339 --> 00:37:19.819
So it's not small enough for you to carry
around in a USB stick.

00:37:19.819 --> 00:37:22.589
It's not small enough for you to download
it

00:37:22.589 --> 00:37:24.640
in a FTP server.

00:37:24.640 --> 00:37:30.849
We tried, and it took us more than a few weeks,
and the transfer never succeeded because these

00:37:30.849 --> 00:37:34.079
are inherently unreliable ways of moving data.

00:37:34.079 --> 00:37:39.900
So we built a service that makes it easy for
you to manage, share, and transfer your datasets.

00:37:39.900 --> 00:37:41.680
It's called Globus.

00:37:41.680 --> 00:37:54.220
Globus is a data management service that makes
it easy for researchers to move Big Data easily

00:37:54.220 --> 00:37:59.450
without having
to become experts in Unix.

00:37:59.450 --> 00:38:05.299
It also makes it easy for researchers to share
datasets with each other reliably.

00:38:05.299 --> 00:38:08.990
In the
later part of the talk I'll talk about some

00:38:08.990 --> 00:38:13.960
of the additional services we built where
researchers can publish their

00:38:13.960 --> 00:38:20.420
results and others can discover them using
some of the management services we created

00:38:20.420 --> 00:38:21.530
and operate.

00:38:21.530 --> 00:38:34.500
So as I said before, Globus makes it easy
for researchers to do large-scale data transfers.

00:38:34.500 --> 00:38:38.790
It provides secure
access with the multi-tier security model.

00:38:38.790 --> 00:38:42.240
And one of the important things that we've
done is we have improved the

00:38:42.240 --> 00:38:44.760
performance with which you can move data.

00:38:44.760 --> 00:38:47.380
So you can move a lot of data really quickly.

00:38:47.380 --> 00:38:53.020
So this is a graphic that
shows improved performance and comparison

00:38:53.020 --> 00:38:57.900
with existing data streams, with existing
data protocols, and how

00:38:57.900 --> 00:39:00.840
quickly you can move data.

00:39:00.840 --> 00:39:09.250
So, as you can see, GridFTP, which is a protocol
that we use to move data in Globus,

00:39:09.250 --> 00:39:16.670
outperforms traditional data transfer protocols.

00:39:16.670 --> 00:39:20.550
So this is an interface for searching.

00:39:20.550 --> 00:39:26.030
This is a Big Data repository where researchers
can publish their datasets

00:39:26.030 --> 00:39:29.950
with metadata and others can look into it
and discover them.

00:39:29.950 --> 00:39:34.190
And when I talk about that the TReNA pipeline,
I'll

00:39:34.190 --> 00:39:41.760
show how we have leveraged all of these services,
how we leverage Globus transfer to move the

00:39:41.760 --> 00:39:49.000
raw data from
ENCODE into Amazon cloud storage, and how

00:39:49.000 --> 00:39:52.069
we were able to leverage our analysis service
to run large-scale

00:39:52.069 --> 00:39:57.380
analysis, how we generated minids for them,
and how we generated results and published

00:39:57.380 --> 00:40:03.410
them into these data
repositories for others to find them.

00:40:03.410 --> 00:40:09.880
So this is a process in which somebody would
publish their datasets into the Globus publication

00:40:09.880 --> 00:40:11.319
service.

00:40:11.319 --> 00:40:15.640
So
Globus is pretty widely used across the research

00:40:15.640 --> 00:40:16.640
enterprise.

00:40:16.640 --> 00:40:20.590
Over 190 petabytes of data have been moved.

00:40:20.590 --> 00:40:24.099
20
billion files were processed.

00:40:24.099 --> 00:40:31.470
It has over 50,000 registered users and enjoys
a lot of uptime because we run this as

00:40:31.470 --> 00:40:37.640
a production quality service that the researchers
can depend on for managing their Big Data

00:40:37.640 --> 00:40:43.760
.
So lets move to the how, now that we figured

00:40:43.760 --> 00:40:48.770
out a way to generate unique identifiers,
to generate portable data

00:40:48.770 --> 00:40:58.609
formats for these raw ENCODE datasets, of
all of this is in support of finding those

00:40:58.609 --> 00:40:59.900
needles in a Haystack.

00:40:59.900 --> 00:41:03.480
So we
built this service called Globus Genomics

00:41:03.480 --> 00:41:09.480
that combines Globus data management and analysis
on the cloud

00:41:09.480 --> 00:41:13.960
using a tool called Galaxy.

00:41:13.960 --> 00:41:23.599
So using this service, researchers can get
the data bags that they have generated from

00:41:23.599 --> 00:41:25.630
ENCODE service.

00:41:25.630 --> 00:41:29.910
They
can build a scalable analysis pipeline that

00:41:29.910 --> 00:41:33.859
runs on all cloud computing resources.

00:41:33.859 --> 00:41:39.809
One of the important innovations
that we have done is that by understanding

00:41:39.809 --> 00:41:44.039
the computational capabilities of these tools,
we were able to schedule

00:41:44.039 --> 00:41:53.210
these analyses to be run on cloud resources
at a very small price using spot market.

00:41:53.210 --> 00:41:58.180
So you could do large-scale
Big Data analysis without having to pay a

00:41:58.180 --> 00:42:03.289
lot of dollars for compute.

00:42:03.289 --> 00:42:10.840
So how do we, now that we have seen all of
these building blocks of doing Big Data analysis,

00:42:10.840 --> 00:42:15.490
bring them all to
bear for validating this one hypothesis that

00:42:15.490 --> 00:42:17.589
I presented before?

00:42:17.589 --> 00:42:20.940
So we created a small chart that explains
how

00:42:20.940 --> 00:42:22.520
we've done it.

00:42:22.520 --> 00:42:31.510
We created a interface that will allow researchers
to query the ENCODE dataset, create a bag,

00:42:31.510 --> 00:42:36.190
and
use that bag to send that to the Globus genomic

00:42:36.190 --> 00:42:38.299
service that I showed before.

00:42:38.299 --> 00:42:45.079
And the service will allow
researchers to assemble a pipeline of analysis

00:42:45.079 --> 00:42:49.900
that will take this BDBag, do the analysis
at scale on a cloud

00:42:49.900 --> 00:42:57.640
computing resource, and generate a bag of
results that show how these transcriptomic

00:42:57.640 --> 00:43:04.589
regulatory networks are
affecting various genetic loci for Alzheimer's

00:43:04.589 --> 00:43:05.589
disease.

00:43:05.589 --> 00:43:11.670
We take these networks and we publish them
in a publication service and we take the raw

00:43:11.670 --> 00:43:19.349
data that we generate
and publish them into the DERIVA service that

00:43:19.349 --> 00:43:22.930
University of Southern California manages
so others can discover

00:43:22.930 --> 00:43:26.960
them and query them easily.

00:43:26.960 --> 00:43:32.230
So just to remind you, this is a pipeline
that we are attempting to build in the Globus

00:43:32.230 --> 00:43:33.359
genomic service.

00:43:33.359 --> 00:43:38.359
We ended
up creating one which takes-- this is pipeline

00:43:38.359 --> 00:43:46.520
in the bottom half of the picture --and it
takes the minid as an input,

00:43:46.520 --> 00:43:52.730
the minid is the ID for the BDBag that we
have generated from the ENCODE data repository.

00:43:52.730 --> 00:43:58.520
So this minid could
represent all of the raw datasets for a particular

00:43:58.520 --> 00:44:01.230
organ that a researcher is interested in.

00:44:01.230 --> 00:44:05.680
We run this analysis at
scale, and we generate the transcriptomic

00:44:05.680 --> 00:44:10.170
regulatory network model that will help researchers
to get a better

00:44:10.170 --> 00:44:18.329
understanding of how these genetic loci factor
in the onset of Alzheimer's.

00:44:18.329 --> 00:44:21.849
So this is how we run it at scale on all of
the datasets.

00:44:21.849 --> 00:44:25.859
And I have a slide that shows the scale at
which we run it.

00:44:25.859 --> 00:44:33.270
So once we have these results, we can essentially
do, we generated a bag of results.

00:44:33.270 --> 00:44:38.960
So this bag is created by a
colleague of mine at the University of Chicago.

00:44:38.960 --> 00:44:43.890
As I have shown before, that the bag of raw
input was generated

00:44:43.890 --> 00:44:48.849
by Kyle, but the bag of results is created
by Alex.

00:44:48.849 --> 00:44:52.650
What was the BDBag allows you to do is that
multiple

00:44:52.650 --> 00:44:59.450
collaborators can work and be able to exchange
datasets with ease with each other with enough

00:44:59.450 --> 00:45:00.450
metadata.

00:45:00.450 --> 00:45:07.349
So this particular bag refers to lymphoblast
cell, with the footprinting results from those,

00:45:07.349 --> 00:45:12.740
or the transcriptomic
regulatory networks for that particular tissue.

00:45:12.740 --> 00:45:19.910
We also built a catalog that makes it easy
for researchers to query these results.

00:45:19.910 --> 00:45:29.329
So for example, within ENCODE
we have generated results for 26 tissue types.

00:45:29.329 --> 00:45:35.339
And these results span from genetic sets,
proteomic datasets, but if

00:45:35.339 --> 00:45:44.410
a researcher is trying to narrow down a particular
area of interest, it's often hard for them

00:45:44.410 --> 00:45:50.420
to sift through
thousands of files in order to find that data.

00:45:50.420 --> 00:45:57.349
We built a catalog called DERIVA that catalogs
using the metadata of all of these datasets,

00:45:57.349 --> 00:46:01.450
catalogs all the data,
and provides a nice and easy-to-use interface

00:46:01.450 --> 00:46:05.430
that allows researchers to query for particular
aspects of the data

00:46:05.430 --> 00:46:09.339
and do further downstream analysis of this
data.

00:46:09.339 --> 00:46:13.299
I talked about the data repository before.

00:46:13.299 --> 00:46:21.529
This is another mechanism for publishing the
findings of a research

00:46:21.529 --> 00:46:22.529
paper.

00:46:22.529 --> 00:46:27.819
If somebody is reading your paper, we will
provide the links to a repository.

00:46:27.819 --> 00:46:32.779
They can go to this repository
to find the results.

00:46:32.779 --> 00:46:37.890
That helps them to build on your research
or on your findings.

00:46:37.890 --> 00:46:50.180
So, in conclusion, with this particular analysis,
there are 26 tissue types, but they correspond

00:46:50.180 --> 00:46:51.599
to 206 tissue
samples.

00:46:51.599 --> 00:46:58.230
The total amount of data we have analyzed
is around 2.5 terabytes.

00:46:58.230 --> 00:47:07.520
We created a lot of tools and highperformance
computing workflows that ran at scale on Amazon

00:47:07.520 --> 00:47:09.680
cloud resources.

00:47:09.680 --> 00:47:16.089
And the important thing in this slide is that
we created 106 publicly available databases

00:47:16.089 --> 00:47:20.330
that provide this regulated
reference for all of the tissue samples that

00:47:20.330 --> 00:47:24.180
are available in code.

00:47:24.180 --> 00:47:30.990
This will help others to build on using these
databases without recreating them.

00:47:30.990 --> 00:47:42.820
So, in conclusion, the BDDS center is comprised
of experts in various life sciences research

00:47:42.820 --> 00:47:44.760
and computer
science.

00:47:44.760 --> 00:47:51.220
Our goal is to provide a streamlined approach
for Big Data management, aggregation, manipulation,

00:47:51.220 --> 00:48:00.359
integration, and modeling of various biological
systems across patients and temporal scales.

00:48:00.359 --> 00:48:06.630
So thank you for taking time to think today,
and I'd be happy to answer any questions that

00:48:06.630 --> 00:48:08.359
you may have.

00:48:08.359 --> 00:48:09.410
Thank you so much Ravi.

00:48:09.410 --> 00:48:15.190
That was a really great overview of a very
interesting ecosystem for being able to

00:48:15.190 --> 00:48:19.829
manage, manipulate, and process large-scale
biomedical data.

00:48:19.829 --> 00:48:23.200
I encourage everybody who's listening to submit
a

00:48:23.200 --> 00:48:27.309
question, again using the go-to webinar question
submission system.

00:48:27.309 --> 00:48:33.290
In the meantime, Ravi, you mentioned something
about the bags, the BDBags system.

00:48:33.290 --> 00:48:37.380
I'm curious how the bag
system differentiates itself from just like

00:48:37.380 --> 00:48:39.769
a classic kind of TAR file?

00:48:39.769 --> 00:48:46.799
And if those are published someplace and they
act as kind of a centralized thing, what happens

00:48:46.799 --> 00:48:51.349
if somebody updates those files and then all
of a sudden, the data

00:48:51.349 --> 00:48:54.390
that I was expecting is now different?

00:48:54.390 --> 00:48:58.640
How is that documented and reported back to
the end users?

00:48:58.640 --> 00:49:00.100
Yeah, that's a great question.

00:49:00.100 --> 00:49:08.530
So one of the important differences between
a .tar file and a bag is that the bag, in

00:49:08.530 --> 00:49:13.619
addition to containing the list of the raw
datasets, it also contains checksums for all

00:49:13.619 --> 00:49:17.619
of those datasets, and a
checksum for the bag itself.

00:49:17.619 --> 00:49:26.359
So for example, if you change the file or
if you change the collection that the bag

00:49:26.359 --> 00:49:30.270
refers to, the checksums don't
match anymore.

00:49:30.270 --> 00:49:38.849
So if somebody were to download a TAR file
today, and the generator of the TAR file changed

00:49:38.849 --> 00:49:44.920
that to a different-- added or deleted some
data in that TAR file --if you download it

00:49:44.920 --> 00:49:52.000
tomorrow, you could look at
both the TAR files and they would be the same,

00:49:52.000 --> 00:49:58.069
but when you include the checksum within the
TAR file, you can

00:49:58.069 --> 00:50:02.300
say that yes, this file has changed since
the last time I touched it.

00:50:02.300 --> 00:50:06.410
So that's one big differentiator between a
bag

00:50:06.410 --> 00:50:08.030
and a TAR file.

00:50:08.030 --> 00:50:13.000
The second thing is that a bag also has a
minid associated with it.

00:50:13.000 --> 00:50:18.210
This is a unique identifier that has more
metadata associated with it, in terms of who

00:50:18.210 --> 00:50:23.970
created this bag, where is this bag located,
when was this created,

00:50:23.970 --> 00:50:29.480
and the checksum for the data that was in
that bag.

00:50:29.480 --> 00:50:35.780
This makes it a lot more powerful when you
care about the

00:50:35.780 --> 00:50:37.099
reproducibility of research.

00:50:37.099 --> 00:50:43.960
Also, are there any dependencies on the type
of workflow engine that's being deployed?

00:50:43.960 --> 00:50:48.230
So I mean you
mentioned Galaxy, obviously, which is widely

00:50:48.230 --> 00:50:51.910
popular for genetics and for other things.

00:50:51.910 --> 00:50:56.789
I'm curious if other
workflow engines are suitable here.

00:50:56.789 --> 00:51:00.530
Can describe anything that's been done with
other systems?

00:51:00.530 --> 00:51:03.369
Yeah, no, that's a great question.

00:51:03.369 --> 00:51:10.830
So no, so the bag, the BDBag tool and the
format is not dependent on one

00:51:10.830 --> 00:51:13.119
workflow.

00:51:13.119 --> 00:51:21.039
So we have work done to make the LONI pipeline,
which is a very popular tool to do large-scale

00:51:21.039 --> 00:51:23.030
image
analysis.

00:51:23.030 --> 00:51:33.700
They have used the BDBag format and tools
to exchange large image datasets.

00:51:33.700 --> 00:51:39.170
So it's not just
constrained to a tool or a technology, it

00:51:39.170 --> 00:51:44.460
is a pretty general purpose tool.

00:51:44.460 --> 00:51:46.530
That's great.

00:51:46.530 --> 00:51:49.519
Send in any questions that you might have,
folks.

00:51:49.519 --> 00:51:53.500
One other thing I was curious about is, where
are these jobs run?

00:51:53.500 --> 00:52:00.420
I mean, I presume that at University of Chicago
and at Argonne, you've got giant computers,

00:52:00.420 --> 00:52:03.599
warehouses full of computers where this stuff
can run.

00:52:03.599 --> 00:52:06.599
Are these the
types of things which can be deployed to other

00:52:06.599 --> 00:52:12.940
institutions, or is it solely dependent upon
your cluster?

00:52:12.940 --> 00:52:18.520
Or could it
run on my Amazon account or my Google Cloud

00:52:18.520 --> 00:52:19.730
accounts?

00:52:19.730 --> 00:52:29.579
Yes, so as I mentioned, one of the things
that we've done in our work is we realize

00:52:29.579 --> 00:52:34.000
that we don't want to build a
haystack sorting machine for everybody.

00:52:34.000 --> 00:52:39.190
I mean, to some extent, if everybody decides
to build their own machine,

00:52:39.190 --> 00:52:42.570
then it takes a long time to do science.

00:52:42.570 --> 00:52:49.309
So all of the tools, Globus and Globus genomics
that are all services that are running on

00:52:49.309 --> 00:52:50.880
Amazon.

00:52:50.880 --> 00:52:57.000
And if you want
to run it on your own, at least Globus genomics,

00:52:57.000 --> 00:53:00.069
you can run it in your Amazon account.

00:53:00.069 --> 00:53:08.910
So there are some
operational tools that we have built to make

00:53:08.910 --> 00:53:14.290
these services reliable and make them run
at scale on public cloud

00:53:14.290 --> 00:53:15.809
computing resources.

00:53:15.809 --> 00:53:22.329
And where can we find these resources if we're
interested in investigating them ourselves?

00:53:22.329 --> 00:53:33.319
I should have put a link for to the BDDS tools
web page.

00:53:33.319 --> 00:53:36.160
I should have put it in my slides.

00:53:36.160 --> 00:53:42.200
But the BDDS center
website has a tools section where you can

00:53:42.200 --> 00:53:48.500
find all of the tools and GitHub repositories
where they are located.

00:53:48.500 --> 00:53:55.990
Fantastic, and where can we find Globus and
the Globus tools?

00:53:55.990 --> 00:54:00.680
Globus.org is the website.

00:54:00.680 --> 00:54:04.060
And this is sort of a little bit of a different
question.

00:54:04.060 --> 00:54:08.329
In the context, I mean we have these webinars
in the context of

00:54:08.329 --> 00:54:14.130
biomedical data science, but I'm curious if
through Globus and through other initiatives

00:54:14.130 --> 00:54:17.819
that you guys are involved
with there at the University of Chicago and

00:54:17.819 --> 00:54:23.210
with Argonne, that you must be dealing with
other scientific data types,

00:54:23.210 --> 00:54:29.440
whether it's particle physics or astrophysics
or something else that generates massive-scale

00:54:29.440 --> 00:54:30.440
data.

00:54:30.440 --> 00:54:32.960
I'm curious if,
do you have any lessons learned from those

00:54:32.960 --> 00:54:37.339
data types or those scientific domains which
are helpful as

00:54:37.339 --> 00:54:42.670
biomedical scientists deal with the ever-increasingly
large datasets?

00:54:42.670 --> 00:54:45.240
Yeah, definitely.

00:54:45.240 --> 00:54:51.119
One of the great things about working at Argonne
and University of Chicago is that there are

00:54:51.119 --> 00:54:55.660
a lot
of great sciences that happened here.

00:54:55.660 --> 00:54:59.750
Traditionally we have worked very closely
with the Large Hadron Collider

00:54:59.750 --> 00:55:04.000
on particle physics, on discovery of Higgs-Boson.

00:55:04.000 --> 00:55:09.700
And a lot of lessons learned in Big Data management,
and highenergy

00:55:09.700 --> 00:55:19.519
physics, and on earthquake sciences, and climate
modeling, those were sort of the lessons that

00:55:19.519 --> 00:55:23.950
have
resulted in the tools and services that we've

00:55:23.950 --> 00:55:24.950
built.

00:55:24.950 --> 00:55:31.319
It's interesting you say, so in the high [INAUDIBLE]
physics there is one central location in CERN

00:55:31.319 --> 00:55:33.470
where Big Data is
being generated.

00:55:33.470 --> 00:55:37.789
This particle collider is generating petabytes
of data.

00:55:37.789 --> 00:55:42.509
And one of the early things that they have
done is every institution in the world that

00:55:42.509 --> 00:55:48.109
is doing research in particle physics started
building their own

00:55:48.109 --> 00:55:49.109
infrastructure.

00:55:49.109 --> 00:55:56.980
They quickly realized that there's no way
they can host all the data they need.

00:55:56.980 --> 00:55:59.080
They can't run all the
compute they need.

00:55:59.080 --> 00:56:08.230
They created what is called a [INAUDIBLE]
grid, which is a distributed multi-tier model

00:56:08.230 --> 00:56:13.920
that
has streamlined managing data, moving data

00:56:13.920 --> 00:56:18.650
to multiple centers across the world, and
distributing the compute

00:56:18.650 --> 00:56:20.560
that is required to gain the insights.

00:56:20.560 --> 00:56:27.779
If you been following the press lately, we
were involved in building infrastructure for

00:56:27.779 --> 00:56:34.200
LIGO, which was in the press
recently for discovering gravitational waves.

00:56:34.200 --> 00:56:37.490
And this is a similar approach that the gravitational
physicists have

00:56:37.490 --> 00:56:42.099
taken in order to gain insights from the data
they're generating.

00:56:42.099 --> 00:56:47.990
I realize, you know life sciences, they're
still, in the

00:56:47.990 --> 00:56:54.119
grand scale of Big Data sciences, life sciences
is sort of a newcomer, especially with the

00:56:54.119 --> 00:57:04.070
advent of next-Gen
sequencing and large-scale MRI machines that

00:57:04.070 --> 00:57:07.170
are generating a lot more data.

00:57:07.170 --> 00:57:15.020
But this is the expertise that we gathered
from working with various other domains.

00:57:15.020 --> 00:57:21.730
And we think it has a lot of
impact and use in the life sciences, too.

00:57:21.730 --> 00:57:26.599
We don't have to relearn every lesson that
is learned across all the other

00:57:26.599 --> 00:57:31.970
domains just because we have an un-inventive
attitude.

00:57:31.970 --> 00:57:34.770
Our goal is to sort of bring some of the lessons
that we

00:57:34.770 --> 00:57:40.140
learned from other domains to bring to bear,
to accelerate the discovery process in life

00:57:40.140 --> 00:57:46.339
sciences, mostly because
the discoveries that we make in life sciences

00:57:46.339 --> 00:57:49.780
have a great impact in human health.

00:57:49.780 --> 00:57:51.270
That makes it very personal.

00:57:51.270 --> 00:57:59.799
Oh, absolutely, I think the holy grail of
large-scale data science for biomedicine is

00:57:59.799 --> 00:58:06.450
to do something like discover a
Higgs-Boson, or find gravitational waves,

00:58:06.450 --> 00:58:12.450
or have some big results that could not have
existed unless we had

00:58:12.450 --> 00:58:18.750
marshaled massive amounts of data and figured
out how to leverage large-scale computing,

00:58:18.750 --> 00:58:24.230
move data only when
needed, apply workflow technologies, and to

00:58:24.230 --> 00:58:27.260
be able to model things such that we can make
those kinds of

00:58:27.260 --> 00:58:28.260
discoveries.

00:58:28.260 --> 00:58:29.670
That would be a wonderful outcome.

00:58:29.670 --> 00:58:33.250
And I think what you've described here looks
like we're well

00:58:33.250 --> 00:58:38.769
down the path of getting towards that special
day when something like that happens and we

00:58:38.769 --> 00:58:44.940
cure cancer, or we
get some insight into disease or diabetes,

00:58:44.940 --> 00:58:46.089
or something else.

00:58:46.089 --> 00:58:55.660
So thank you for sharing this exciting ecosystem
for discovery science, Ravi, really appreciate

00:58:55.660 --> 00:58:56.849
it.

00:58:56.849 --> 00:58:59.790
And with that
we've kind of reached the end of our hour.

00:58:59.790 --> 00:59:04.140
I want to thank everyone for tuning in, and
please look forward to

00:59:04.140 --> 00:59:13.930
future speakers and our BD2K guide to the
fundamentals of data science.

00:59:13.930 --> 00:59:15.259
We'll see you next time.

00:59:15.259 --> 00:59:18.470
Thank you
everyone, and thank you Ravi.

00:59:18.470 --> 00:59:18.790
Thank you.

